-- discofetch-fetchpoint-lib -- serving a FetchPoint: the verbs, the rooms,
-- the rendezvous reads. Extracted from api/supervisor.lua (the serving
-- banner, the rooms tier, serve_fetchpoint) without changing arithmetic,
-- branch order or refusal behaviour.
--
-- This is the seam doc/ROADMAP.md Phase 5 fills in. It is deliberately in
-- the ROOT program rather than in a spawned guest, and that is not a
-- shortcut: capability_testing/cap5 established that the listener delivers
-- to the root instance, so a spawned child cannot receive HTTP at all on
-- this host build. cap4 established the matching half -- there is no
-- point-to-point messaging between instances, so children coordinate
-- through shared host state (the database) rather than a bus. Those two
-- findings together are why a FetchPoint is a ROUTE here and not a process.
--
-- TRUST. The dispatcher picks a FetchPoint off the Host header. The Host is
-- chosen by the caller and nothing upstream verifies it, so it SELECTS a
-- FetchPoint and confers NOTHING. Every authority question below is
-- answered by the fetchpoint's own config and its own tokens, never by the
-- name used to arrive. The library never parses a Host: it is handed the
-- label and the verb.
--
-- ===========================================================================
-- SURFACE
--
-- 1. ENTRY POINTS
--
--    M.new(deps)                      -> instance (deps contract below)
--    M.valid_room(name)               pure: the join/read/ice/outcome contract
--    M.ice_defaults(zone)             pure: a fresh ICE table for one zone
--
--    inst:serve_fetchpoint(conn, req, label, verb)   the whole door
--
--    inst:room_prune(fp_id, room, ttl, t)
--    inst:room_list(fp_id, room, ttl, t)          -> newest-first list
--    inst:room_join(fp_id, room, ttl, cap, cand, t) -> list | nil, code, msg
--    inst:room_get(fp_id, room, ttl, key, t)      -> entry | nil
--    inst:room_entry_json(c)                      -> the wire shape, as text
--    inst:room_allowed(cfg, req)                  -> boolean
--    inst:rooms_count()                           -> count, cap
--
--    inst:traffic_bump(fp_id, field)
--    inst:traffic_of(fp_id)                       -> counters (zeros, never nil)
--    inst:traffic_totals()                        -> totals, names
--    inst:traffic_each()                          -> pairs() over live rows
--
--    THE DEPS CONTRACT. Everything that reaches the host, the db, the clock
--    or another library in this set arrives here, and a missing or
--    wrong-typed dep is a NAMED FAILURE at new() rather than a nil deref on
--    the hot serving path:
--
--      now              function -> unix SECONDS. Never host.time(): a
--                       millisecond clock changes every rate by 1000x.
--      ready            function -> ok, error_sentence. A PREDICATE, never a
--                       captured boolean -- db_ready flips after migrate()
--                       and a snapshot taken at construction freezes false.
--      db               the drt sql connector handle (query/exec)
--      rows_by_name     drt-db-lib: a connector result read by column name
--      json             encode/decode
--      http             drt-http-api-lib: reply, fail (instance methods) and
--                       observed_address, observed_port, public_forwarded
--      model            discofetch-model-lib: KINDS, DIM, valid_ip
--      db_ops           discofetch-db-lib: advertise(fp_id, addr, t) -> at, changed
--      rate             token-rate-limit-lib: check(key, policy, what) -> nil
--                       or a refusal {status, code, message, headers}, and
--                       POLICIES.authfail
--      tier             discofetch-accounts-lib tier_of(name) -> the tier row.
--                       The OWNER's, because whose name it is decides how
--                       fast it may be exercised, not who is calling.
--      zone             'discofetch.link' -- deployment config, not ours
--      turn_credential  optional; absent, a turn=true room answers the same
--                       503 turn_unavailable a failed mint answers
--      ice              optional overrides over M.ice_defaults(zone)
--      room_auth        optional extra policies over M.ROOM_AUTH
--
--    NOT injected, on purpose: the traffic meter is this library's (it has
--    no other writer), and BOOT_AT stays in the composition root -- it is a
--    process-lifetime fact, not the meter's.
--
-- 2. CONFIGURABLE VALUES
--
--    ROOMS_MAX_PER_FP / ROOMS_GLOBAL_MAX   the rooms store's two caps
--    LIMITS                                what a join may carry
--    M.ice_defaults(zone)                  STUN/TURN/vantages and the ICE caps
--    TRAFFIC_FIELDS                        the counters, spelled once
--
-- 3. FAN-OUT POINTS
--
--    ROOM_AUTH   room policy, per the owner's config. OPEN to extension: a
--                fourth policy is a registration (deps.room_auth), not a new
--                elseif. Unknown modes fall back to 'key'.
--    VERB        <label>--<verb> dispatch, by method name. Anything the
--                kind registry lists that is not dns/ice/outcome is a join,
--                which is how it has always behaved.
--    The bare-name answer is a MATRIX, not a dispatch -- four outcomes over
--    two dimensions, and the order of the tests is load-bearing:
--      1. report + caller                        -> serve_reflect
--      2. (report + self) or (redirect + no target) -> serve_rendezvous
--      3. redirect (i.e. with a target)          -> serve_static_redirect
--      4. anything else                          -> 501 kind_not_implemented
--
-- ===========================================================================
-- THE ROOMS STORE: MEMORY, ON PURPOSE. Room candidates are presence, not
-- history -- Bob's laptop at the atrium-west AP for the next five minutes --
-- and presence with a TTL is restart-tolerant by construction: every member
-- re-joins on an interval, so losing the table to a deploy costs one refresh
-- cycle of visibility and zero facts. What memory buys is the spike: a
-- conference's top-of-the-hour stampede becomes table writes in the root
-- program's own heap instead of a database transaction per hello. What it
-- PROMISES is better: these addresses never touch disk. Two invariants keep
-- that promise true -- the deployment must never enable hibernation for the
-- root instance (a snapshot would persist what we said never lands), and
-- nothing in this library ever writes a candidate through the sql connector.
-- The three room functions ARE the storage seam: a 'durable' room tier later
-- is a second implementation behind them, chosen per fetchpoint by a
-- retention config field, not a rewrite.
--
-- Shape: rooms[fp_id][room][addr..'#'..port] = {address, port, name, at}.
-- Pruning is lazy (on touch) plus capacity-checked on join; the global cap is
-- a memory backstop far above any honest use.
-- ===========================================================================

local M = {}

M.ROOMS_MAX_PER_FP = 256       -- distinct rooms one fetchpoint may hold
M.ROOMS_GLOBAL_MAX = 100000    -- candidates across everything; backstop

-- What a join may carry, and what a room hands back. room_ttl and
-- room_capacity are per-fetchpoint config; these are the defaults a config
-- that says nothing gets.
M.LIMITS = {
  room_ttl        = 300,
  room_capacity   = 16,
  port_min        = 1,
  port_max        = 65535,
  name_max        = 32,       -- a candidate name, [%w%-_ .]
  ufrag_min       = 4,
  ufrag_max       = 256,
  pwd_min         = 22,
  pwd_max         = 256,
  wg_pubkey_len   = 44,       -- 32 bytes of base64, ending in '='
  rtt_ms_max      = 60000,
}

-- What actually happened, per fetchpoint, since the last restart. reads
-- counts bare-name requests whatever their outcome; dns_updates and joins
-- count what LANDED; denied and rate_limited count the refusals among them.
-- ice/turn are credentials handed out; direct/relay_reported/failed are what
-- peers REPORTED -- nothing here is observed, and the room read says silent
-- for the rest. The window is honest rather than impressive: these answer
-- "is my scheduled task running" and "is someone poking at my door", not
-- billing. One list, because the bump and the totals are two readers of the
-- same fact and two copies drift.
M.TRAFFIC_FIELDS = { 'reads', 'dns_updates', 'joins', 'denied', 'rate_limited',
                     'ice', 'turn', 'direct', 'relay_reported', 'failed' }

-- Who may join or read a room, per the owner's config. 'open' is the
-- secret-meeting-id pattern when paired with an unguessable room name; 'pin'
-- is a human-typeable secret; 'key' is the owner's own credential -- and 'key'
-- is also what an unrecognised mode gets, because a policy nobody implemented
-- must close the door rather than open it.
local function bearer(req)
  return req.authorization and req.authorization:match('^[Bb]earer%s+(.+)$')
end

M.ROOM_AUTH = {
  open = function(cfg, req) return true end,
  pin  = function(cfg, req)
    return cfg.room_pin ~= nil and req.query.pin == cfg.room_pin
  end,
  key  = function(cfg, req)
    local key = bearer(req) or req.query.key
    return cfg.advertise_key ~= nil and key == cfg.advertise_key
  end,
}

-- The subdomain-suffix surface: <label>--<verb>.<zone>. The kind registry
-- gates which verbs a row answers at all (404 no_verb); this table says which
-- code serves the ones that get through. ice and outcome share a function
-- because they share a gate.
M.VERB = { dns = 'verb_dns', ice = 'verb_ice_or_outcome',
           outcome = 'verb_ice_or_outcome' }
M.VERB_DEFAULT = 'verb_join'

-- Answers about the present moment must not be cached, and the read views
-- carry CORS because a browser on someone else's page is half the audience:
-- a JS snippet asking "what do I look like" or "where is the home box" is
-- exactly the product.
M.PUBLIC_READ = { ['cache-control'] = 'no-store',
                  ['access-control-allow-origin'] = '*' }
M.NO_STORE = { ['cache-control'] = 'no-store' }

-- ICE (doc/ICE.md): what the --ice verb hands an RTCPeerConnection. STUN is
-- public and free; TURN is metered per name, which is why its URL sits here
-- and its credential is minted per request, attributed to the label. The TURN
-- name does not resolve yet -- it is the name the deployment WILL use -- and
-- turn=true is refused on an open room at config time rather than at runtime
-- by surprise.
--
-- A fresh table per call, so two instances cannot share one mutable default.
-- VANTAGES is the one field a self-hoster MUST replace: the others follow the
-- zone, these are two specific edges' addresses.
function M.ice_defaults(zone)
  local ice = {
    -- The STUN pair, spelled once: RTCPeerConnection wants `stun:` URLs
    -- (STUN_URLS, below) and `drt netcheck --stun` wants host:port, and two
    -- lists of the same two servers is how they drift apart.
    STUN_HOSTS = { 'stun1.' .. zone .. ':3478', 'stun2.' .. zone .. ':3478' },
    -- The edges' ADDRESSES, as a caller must reach them to compare two
    -- vantages: gate1, gate2 (deploy/gates/ns/README.md, NETCHECK-SPEC.md
    -- section 4). Names would not do -- reflect's name resolves to both, and
    -- two fetches of it can land on one edge twice. Answered in every JSON
    -- reflect (`measure`, diluvium-drt #25 section 1) so
    -- `drt netcheck --reflect <url>` is the whole invocation: the edge says
    -- how to measure against it, and the operator stops being the transport
    -- for addresses that live in a document. When an edge moves, this moves
    -- with the zone file.
    VANTAGES = { '108.165.121.165', '172.237.132.87' },
    TURN_URLS = { 'turn:turn.' .. zone .. ':3478?transport=udp' },
    TURN_TTL = 3600,            -- seconds a minted TURN credential lives
    MAX_CANDIDATES = 12,        -- per peer, doc/ICE.md 3
    MAX_CANDIDATE_BYTES = 256,  -- each, encoded
    CAND_TYPES = { host = true, srflx = true, prflx = true, relay = true },
    PATHS = { direct = true, relay = true, failed = true },
  }
  ice.STUN_URLS = { 'stun:' .. ice.STUN_HOSTS[1], 'stun:' .. ice.STUN_HOSTS[2] }
  return ice
end

function M.valid_room(name)
  return type(name) == 'string' and #name >= 1 and #name <= 64
     and name:match('^[%w%-_]+$') ~= nil
end

-- depth: construction. Every assertion below names the dep and what it is
-- for, because the alternative is a nil call three branches into a request
-- that a self-hoster then reports as "rooms are broken".
local function need(v, kind, sentence)
  assert(type(v) == kind, 'fetchpoint.new: ' .. sentence)
  return v
end

local function needs_fns(t, names, what)
  assert(type(t) == 'table', 'fetchpoint.new: ' .. what)
  for _, n in ipairs(names) do
    assert(type(t[n]) == 'function', 'fetchpoint.new: ' .. what
      .. ' -- it must provide ' .. n .. '()')
  end
  return t
end

function M.new(deps)
  assert(type(deps) == 'table', 'fetchpoint.new: deps must be a table')
  local self = setmetatable({}, { __index = M })

  self.now = need(deps.now, 'function',
    'deps.now must be a function returning unix SECONDS (never host.time, '
    .. 'which answers milliseconds)')
  self.ready = need(deps.ready, 'function',
    'deps.ready must be a predicate returning ok, error_sentence -- not a '
    .. 'captured boolean, because db_ready flips after migrate()')
  self.db = needs_fns(deps.db, { 'query' },
    'deps.db must be the open sql connector')
  self.rows_by_name = need(deps.rows_by_name, 'function',
    'deps.rows_by_name must be drt-db-lib rows_by_name(result)')
  self.json = needs_fns(deps.json, { 'encode', 'decode' },
    'deps.json must be the json codec')
  self.http = needs_fns(deps.http, { 'reply', 'fail', 'observed_address',
    'observed_port', 'public_forwarded' },
    'deps.http must be a drt-http-api-lib instance')
  self.model = needs_fns(deps.model, { 'valid_ip' },
    'deps.model must be discofetch-model-lib')
  assert(type(self.model.KINDS) == 'table',
    'fetchpoint.new: deps.model must carry KINDS, the verb gate and kind registry')
  assert(type(self.model.DIM) == 'table' and type(self.model.DIM.of) == 'function',
    'fetchpoint.new: deps.model must carry DIM.of, which answers the dimensions and the decoded config')
  self.db_ops = needs_fns(deps.db_ops, { 'advertise' },
    'deps.db_ops must be a discofetch-db-lib instance (advertise is its write, '
    .. 'shared with the panel PUT -- one writer for both doors)')
  self.rate = needs_fns(deps.rate, { 'check', 'headers' },
    'deps.rate must be a token-rate-limit-lib instance')
  self.authfail = deps.authfail
    or (type(deps.rate.POLICIES) == 'table' and deps.rate.POLICIES.authfail)
  assert(type(self.authfail) == 'table',
    'fetchpoint.new: the authfail policy must arrive as deps.rate.POLICIES.authfail '
    .. '(or deps.authfail) -- this library must not spell those numbers itself')
  self.tier = need(deps.tier, 'function',
    'deps.tier must be discofetch-accounts-lib tier_of(name); the dns and join '
    .. 'verbs meter at the OWNER tier')
  self.zone = need(deps.zone, 'string',
    'deps.zone must be the serving zone, e.g. discofetch.link')
  assert(#self.zone > 0, 'fetchpoint.new: deps.zone must not be empty')

  if deps.turn_credential ~= nil then
    self.turn_credential = need(deps.turn_credential, 'function',
      'deps.turn_credential must be a function (label, ttl) -> credential; omit '
      .. 'it entirely on a deployment that cannot mint')
  end

  -- Defaults by zone, overridden field by field. A self-hoster changes
  -- VANTAGES and the TURN URL; nobody should have to restate the rest.
  self.ice = M.ice_defaults(self.zone)
  if deps.ice ~= nil then
    assert(type(deps.ice) == 'table', 'fetchpoint.new: deps.ice must be a table of overrides')
    for k, v in pairs(deps.ice) do self.ice[k] = v end
    if deps.ice.STUN_HOSTS and not deps.ice.STUN_URLS then
      self.ice.STUN_URLS = { 'stun:' .. self.ice.STUN_HOSTS[1],
                             'stun:' .. self.ice.STUN_HOSTS[2] }
    end
  end

  -- Per-instance policy registry: two instances coexist, and a deployment's
  -- fourth policy is never visible to the other one.
  self.room_auth = {}
  for k, v in pairs(M.ROOM_AUTH) do self.room_auth[k] = v end
  if deps.room_auth ~= nil then
    assert(type(deps.room_auth) == 'table',
      'fetchpoint.new: deps.room_auth must be a table of policy functions')
    for k, v in pairs(deps.room_auth) do
      assert(type(v) == 'function',
        'fetchpoint.new: room policy ' .. tostring(k) .. ' must be a function (cfg, req)')
      self.room_auth[k] = v
    end
  end

  self.rooms = {}        -- the MEMORY tier; see the banner
  self.rooms_n = 0
  self.traffic = {}
  return self
end

-- depth: the rooms store ---------------------------------------------------

function M:rooms_count()
  -- count AND cap together: a number without the cap it is racing is not
  -- worth printing, and the stats card reads both.
  return self.rooms_n, M.ROOMS_GLOBAL_MAX
end

function M:room_prune(fp_id, room, ttl, t)
  local ROOMS = self.rooms
  local rs = ROOMS[fp_id] and ROOMS[fp_id][room]
  if not rs then return end
  for k, c in pairs(rs) do
    if c.at <= t - ttl then rs[k] = nil; self.rooms_n = self.rooms_n - 1 end
  end
  if next(rs) == nil then ROOMS[fp_id][room] = nil end
  if ROOMS[fp_id] and next(ROOMS[fp_id]) == nil then ROOMS[fp_id] = nil end
end

function M:room_list(fp_id, room, ttl, t)
  self:room_prune(fp_id, room, ttl, t)
  local out = {}
  local rs = self.rooms[fp_id] and self.rooms[fp_id][room]
  if rs then for _, c in pairs(rs) do out[#out + 1] = c end end
  table.sort(out, function(a, b) return a.at > b.at end)
  return out
end

-- Returns the list after the join, or nil plus a refusal.
function M:room_join(fp_id, room, ttl, capacity, cand, t)
  self:room_prune(fp_id, room, ttl, t)
  local ROOMS = self.rooms
  ROOMS[fp_id] = ROOMS[fp_id] or {}
  local fpr = ROOMS[fp_id]
  if not fpr[room] then
    local nrooms = 0
    for _ in pairs(fpr) do nrooms = nrooms + 1 end
    if nrooms >= M.ROOMS_MAX_PER_FP then
      return nil, 'room_limit', 'this fetchpoint holds its maximum number of rooms'
    end
    fpr[room] = {}
  end
  local rs = fpr[room]
  -- A peer-shaped entry (doc/ICE.md 3) is keyed by its ufrag: unique per
  -- session by construction, and what the other side needs anyway. A plain
  -- candidate keeps address#port, so a re-join refreshes it.
  local key = cand.ufrag and ('u#' .. cand.ufrag)
           or (cand.address .. '#' .. tostring(cand.port or 0))
  if not rs[key] then
    local n = 0
    for _ in pairs(rs) do n = n + 1 end
    if n >= capacity then
      return nil, 'room_full', 'this room holds its capacity of ' .. capacity
    end
    if self.rooms_n >= M.ROOMS_GLOBAL_MAX then
      -- Rooms REFUSE the new entry where the rate buckets shed the whole
      -- table: a presence store that silently forgets a swarm is worse than
      -- one that says it is full.
      return nil, 'rooms_saturated', 'the rendezvous store is at its global cap'
    end
    self.rooms_n = self.rooms_n + 1
  end
  rs[key] = cand
  return self:room_list(fp_id, room, ttl, t)
end

function M:room_get(fp_id, room, ttl, key, t)
  self:room_prune(fp_id, room, ttl, t)
  local rs = self.rooms[fp_id] and self.rooms[fp_id][room]
  return rs and rs[key] or nil
end

-- One encoder for both shapes, because the join and the read must show the
-- same thing. A plain candidate encodes as it is. A peer carries an ARRAY of
-- candidates, spliced in by hand: json.encode of an empty Lua table is an
-- object, and a peer with no candidates yet must read as [].
function M:room_entry_json(c)
  local json = self.json
  if not c.ufrag then return json.encode(c) end
  local cj = {}
  for i, cand in ipairs(c.candidates or {}) do cj[i] = json.encode(cand) end
  local head = json.encode({ ufrag = c.ufrag, pwd = c.pwd, name = c.name, at = c.at,
                             observed = c.observed, outcome = c.outcome })
  return head:sub(1, -2) .. ',"candidates":[' .. table.concat(cj, ',') .. ']}'
end

function M:room_allowed(cfg, req)
  local mode = cfg.room_auth or 'key'
  local policy = self.room_auth[mode] or self.room_auth.key
  return policy(cfg, req) and true or false
end

-- depth: the traffic meter -------------------------------------------------

local function zero_counters()
  local t = {}
  for _, k in ipairs(M.TRAFFIC_FIELDS) do t[k] = 0 end
  return t
end

function M:traffic_bump(fp_id, field)
  local tr = self.traffic[fp_id]
  if not tr then
    tr = zero_counters()
    self.traffic[fp_id] = tr
  end
  tr[field] = tr[field] + 1
  tr.last_at = self.now()
end

-- Zeros rather than absence when nothing happened yet: the panel needs
-- numbers, not a shape puzzle.
function M:traffic_of(fp_id)
  local tr = self.traffic[fp_id] or {}
  local out = zero_counters()
  for _, k in ipairs(M.TRAFFIC_FIELDS) do out[k] = tr[k] or 0 end
  out.last_at = tr.last_at
  return out
end

-- The whole meter added up, plus how many ids it holds. `names` counts what
-- has been TOUCHED since boot rather than what exists: a fetchpoint arrives
-- in this table on its first request and a name nobody has called is not in
-- it, which is the honest answer to "what is being hit".
function M:traffic_totals()
  local totals = zero_counters()
  local names = 0
  for _, tr in pairs(self.traffic) do
    names = names + 1
    for k in pairs(totals) do totals[k] = totals[k] + (tr[k] or 0) end
  end
  return totals, names
end

-- The ranker in the admin section iterates; it gets the live rows rather
-- than a copy, and must not write to them.
function M:traffic_each()
  return pairs(self.traffic)
end

-- depth: the shared refusals -----------------------------------------------

-- The name's shared failures bucket, spent by failures ONLY. At 160 bits the
-- advertise key is not guessable in this universe; the throttle is for the
-- log's sake and for uniformity -- every refused door on a name drains the
-- same slow bucket. Without it, wrong-pin guesses ride free under the edge's
-- per-address limit and a four-digit pin falls in an afternoon. Only failures
-- drain it, so a guesser can never lock a right pin out: the right pin never
-- gets here.
function M:refuse_authfail(conn, row)
  self:traffic_bump(row.id, 'denied')
  local ref = self.rate:check('authfail:' .. row.id, self.authfail,
                              'too many refused attempts on this name')
  if ref then
    return self.http:fail(conn, ref.status, ref.code, ref.message, ref.headers), true
  end
  return nil, false
end

local function room_policy_refusal(cfg)
  return 'the room policy (' .. (cfg.room_auth or 'key')
    .. ') did not admit this request'
end

-- depth: the verbs ---------------------------------------------------------

-- The dns verb: advertise-by-showing-up. The address is OBSERVED, never
-- supplied -- the caller cannot claim to be somewhere they are not making
-- this request from. Only the owner may advertise, proven by the
-- per-fetchpoint key (Bearer, or ?key= for clients that can set nothing but
-- a URL).
function M:verb_dns(conn, req, label, row, d, cfg, verb)
  local http = self.http
  local key = bearer(req) or (req.query and req.query.key)
  if not cfg.advertise_key or key ~= cfg.advertise_key then
    local sent, refused = self:refuse_authfail(conn, row)
    if refused then return sent end
    return http:fail(conn, 403, 'bad_advertise_key', 'the advertise key did not match')
  end
  local addr = http.observed_address(req)
  if not addr then
    return http:fail(conn, 400, 'no_observation',
                     'the edge did not forward an observed address')
  end
  -- Metered AFTER auth and observation, so only updates that would otherwise
  -- land spend the bucket: a stranger guessing keys burns the edge's
  -- per-address limit, never the owner's.
  local lim = self.tier(row.tier)
  local ref = self.rate:check('dns:' .. row.id, { lim.dns_per_hour, lim.dns_burst },
    'this name has spent its ' .. lim.dns_per_hour .. ' dns updates for the hour')
  if ref then
    self:traffic_bump(row.id, 'rate_limited')
    return http:fail(conn, ref.status, ref.code, ref.message, ref.headers)
  end
  local t = self.now()
  -- Through db_ops:advertise(), which is where the unchanged-address
  -- heartbeat stops being a durable write (see discofetch-db-lib for the
  -- staleness bound: `at` may run up to ADVERTISE_REFRESH seconds behind the
  -- newest heartbeat). changed:false is a heartbeat, not history -- the DNS
  -- publisher debounces off the same distinction when it lands. `at` is the
  -- row's, not this request's, so the answer never claims a freshness the
  -- readers will not see.
  local stored_at, changed = self.db_ops:advertise(row.id, addr, t)
  local out = { advertised = { address = addr, at = stored_at }, changed = changed }
  -- held says a lock is pinning the name: the observation was recorded and is
  -- not what readers get. Two words for one state, for now: the old rows'
  -- field and the new column.
  if d.published_locked then
    out.held = cfg.address_override and 'address_override' or 'published_locked'
  end
  self:traffic_bump(row.id, 'dns_updates')
  return http:reply(conn, 200, out, M.NO_STORE)
end

-- The ice verb (doc/ICE.md 4): one fetch, ready to paste into an
-- RTCPeerConnection. Behind the room's door, because the TURN credential it
-- may carry spends the owner's relay allowance.
--
-- `ice` needs ice; `outcome` needs only a ROOM. They shared a gate, and the
-- cost was that the path we actually ship could not report itself: a
-- WireGuard peer punches, joins, hands the room its public key, and was then
-- told to PATCH config.ice to say whether it went direct -- a verb that hands
-- out TURN credentials against the owner's relay allowance, switched on to
-- fix a log line. Both ends of the first two-network punch failed here
-- (doc/P2P-CAPABILITY.md 5.2i), which is how it was found.
function M:verb_ice_or_outcome(conn, req, label, row, d, cfg, verb)
  local http, json, ICE = self.http, self.json, self.ice
  if verb == 'ice' and cfg.ice ~= true then
    return http:fail(conn, 400, 'ice_disabled',
                     'this fetchpoint does not serve ice; PATCH config.ice')
  end
  if verb == 'outcome' and not (cfg.rooms == true) then
    return http:fail(conn, 400, 'rooms_disabled',
                     'this fetchpoint does not multiplex rooms; PATCH config.rooms')
  end
  local room = req.query.room or ''
  if room == '' or not M.valid_room(room) then
    return http:fail(conn, 400, 'bad_room',
                     '?room= is required: 1-64 letters, digits, hyphens or underscores')
  end
  if not self:room_allowed(cfg, req) then
    local sent, refused = self:refuse_authfail(conn, row)
    if refused then return sent end
    return http:fail(conn, 403, 'not_allowed', room_policy_refusal(cfg))
  end
  if verb == 'ice' then
    local servers = { json.encode({ urls = ICE.STUN_URLS }) }
    local expires
    if cfg.turn == true then
      -- Minted per request and attributed to the LABEL (doc/ICE.md 5):
      -- labels are published names, so nothing leaks, and an allocation that
      -- cannot be traced to a name cannot be metered.
      local okt, cred
      if self.turn_credential then
        okt, cred = pcall(self.turn_credential, label, ICE.TURN_TTL)
      else
        okt, cred = false, 'no turn_credential was injected'
      end
      if not okt or type(cred) ~= 'table' then
        -- A room configured for TURN on a deployment that cannot mint is a
        -- misconfiguration to surface, not to paper over with a STUN-only
        -- answer that looks like success.
        return http:fail(conn, 503, 'turn_unavailable',
                         'this deployment cannot mint a TURN credential: ' .. tostring(cred))
      end
      servers[#servers + 1] = json.encode({ urls = ICE.TURN_URLS,
        username = cred.username, credential = cred.password })
      expires = cred.expires
      self:traffic_bump(row.id, 'turn')
    end
    self:traffic_bump(row.id, 'ice')
    return http:reply(conn, 200, '{"service":"ice","label":' .. json.encode(label)
      .. ',"room":' .. json.encode(room)
      .. ',"ice_servers":[' .. table.concat(servers, ',') .. ']'
      .. (expires and (',"expires_at":' .. expires) or '') .. '}', M.PUBLIC_READ)
  end
  -- The outcome verb (doc/ICE.md 6): a peer says how it went. REPORTED, not
  -- observed -- the room read counts it apart from silence -- and keyed to a
  -- ufrag currently in the room, so a report at least comes from behind the
  -- door. Nothing here tells two members apart.
  local ufrag, path = req.query.ufrag, req.query.path
  if not ICE.PATHS[path] then
    return http:fail(conn, 400, 'bad_path', 'path is one of direct, relay, failed')
  end
  -- Which member is reporting, addressed the way it JOINED: an ICE peer by
  -- its ufrag, a plain peer by observed address and the port it supplied --
  -- the same key room_join built, so no third naming scheme appears here. A
  -- plain peer cannot know its own observed address, and does not have to:
  -- this edge already does.
  local okey, said
  if ufrag ~= nil and ufrag ~= '' then
    if type(ufrag) ~= 'string' then
      return http:fail(conn, 400, 'bad_ufrag', 'ufrag is a string')
    end
    okey, said = 'u#' .. ufrag, { ufrag = ufrag }
  else
    local oaddr = http.observed_address(req)
    local oport = tonumber(req.query.port or '')
    if not oaddr then
      return http:fail(conn, 400, 'no_observation',
                       'this request carries no usable address to report for')
    end
    if not oport or oport < M.LIMITS.port_min or oport > M.LIMITS.port_max
       or oport % 1 ~= 0 then
      return http:fail(conn, 400, 'bad_port',
                       'port is the one this peer joined with, so the report '
                       .. 'can be matched to it (or send ufrag, for an ICE peer)')
    end
    okey = oaddr .. '#' .. tostring(oport)
    said = { address = oaddr, port = oport }
  end
  local rtt
  if req.query.rtt_ms and req.query.rtt_ms ~= '' then
    rtt = tonumber(req.query.rtt_ms)
    if not rtt or rtt < 0 or rtt > M.LIMITS.rtt_ms_max then
      return http:fail(conn, 400, 'bad_rtt', 'rtt_ms is 0-' .. M.LIMITS.rtt_ms_max)
    end
  end
  local t = self.now()
  local entry = self:room_get(row.id, room, cfg.room_ttl or M.LIMITS.room_ttl, okey, t)
  if not entry then
    return http:fail(conn, 404, 'no_such_peer',
                     'no peer answering to that is in this room')
  end
  entry.outcome = { path = path, rtt_ms = rtt, at = t }
  self:traffic_bump(row.id, path == 'direct' and 'direct'
                            or path == 'relay' and 'relay_reported' or 'failed')
  said.recorded = entry.outcome
  return http:reply(conn, 200, said, M.NO_STORE)
end

-- The join verb: enter a room as a candidate. The address is observed (the
-- same property dns leans on); the PORT may be supplied, because WireGuard
-- needs one and this edge cannot observe UDP. Auth is the room policy the
-- owner configured; capacity and TTL likewise. The candidate goes to MEMORY
-- and nowhere else -- see the rooms tier.
function M:verb_join(conn, req, label, row, d, cfg, verb)
  local http, json, ICE, L = self.http, self.json, self.ice, M.LIMITS
  local room = req.query.room or ''
  if room ~= '' then
    if not (cfg.rooms == true) then
      return http:fail(conn, 400, 'rooms_disabled',
                       'this fetchpoint does not multiplex rooms; PATCH config.rooms')
    end
    if not M.valid_room(room) then
      return http:fail(conn, 400, 'bad_room',
                       'a room is 1-64 letters, digits, hyphens or underscores')
    end
  end
  if not self:room_allowed(cfg, req) then
    local sent, refused = self:refuse_authfail(conn, row)
    if refused then return sent end
    return http:fail(conn, 403, 'not_allowed', room_policy_refusal(cfg))
  end
  -- Per NAME, not per room: capacity already bounds one room, this bounds
  -- what one fetchpoint can push into the shared memory store. It is also
  -- what makes pin and open honest choices to offer. The tier field is per
  -- MINUTE and the bucket is per hour, so the conversion happens here, at the
  -- call site, while the sentence still says per minute.
  local lim = self.tier(row.tier)
  local ref = self.rate:check('join:' .. row.id,
    { lim.joins_per_min * 60, lim.joins_burst },
    'this name has spent its ' .. lim.joins_per_min .. ' joins for the minute')
  if ref then
    self:traffic_bump(row.id, 'rate_limited')
    return http:fail(conn, ref.status, ref.code, ref.message, ref.headers)
  end
  local addr = http.observed_address(req)
  if not addr then
    return http:fail(conn, 400, 'no_observation',
                     'the edge did not forward an observed address')
  end
  local port
  if req.query.port and req.query.port ~= '' then
    port = tonumber(req.query.port)
    if not port or port < L.port_min or port > L.port_max or port % 1 ~= 0 then
      return http:fail(conn, 400, 'bad_port',
                       'port must be an integer ' .. L.port_min .. '-' .. L.port_max)
    end
  end
  local cname
  if req.query.name and req.query.name ~= '' then
    cname = req.query.name
    if #cname > L.name_max or not cname:match('^[%w%-_ .]+$') then
      return http:fail(conn, 400, 'bad_name',
                       'a candidate name is at most ' .. L.name_max
                       .. ' letters, digits, spaces, ., - or _')
    end
  end
  -- A WireGuard peer's two facts ride on a plain candidate (doc/P2P-
  -- CAPABILITY.md: the direct path). The public key is what the other side
  -- configures; the tunnel address is what it routes to. Neither fits in
  -- `name` (32 of [A-Za-z0-9 ._-]; a key is 44 of base64), so they are
  -- fields, validated by shape: a key that is not 32 bytes of base64 is a
  -- device nobody can reach, and refusing it here is the last place the
  -- mistake is cheap.
  local wg_pubkey, wg_address
  if req.query.wg_pubkey and req.query.wg_pubkey ~= '' then
    wg_pubkey = req.query.wg_pubkey
    if #wg_pubkey ~= L.wg_pubkey_len or not wg_pubkey:match('^[%w+/]+=$') then
      return http:fail(conn, 400, 'bad_wg_pubkey',
                       'wg_pubkey is a WireGuard public key: ' .. L.wg_pubkey_len
                       .. ' characters of base64 ending in =')
    end
  end
  if req.query.wg_address and req.query.wg_address ~= '' then
    wg_address = req.query.wg_address
    if not self.model.valid_ip(wg_address) then
      return http:fail(conn, 400, 'bad_wg_address',
                       'wg_address is the tunnel-side IP literal this peer holds')
    end
  end
  -- A peer sending through a TURN allocation is reachable at the relay, not
  -- where the edge observed it from -- the one case where "the address is
  -- observed, never supplied" would publish an address nobody can reach.
  -- `relay` is that endpoint, labelled as what it is (the relay candidate of
  -- a plain peer; an ICE join says the same as a candidate of type relay).
  -- `observed` still says where the request came from, so nothing about the
  -- peer's location is hidden, and a wrong relay address costs only the peer
  -- that supplied it.
  local relay
  if req.query.relay and req.query.relay ~= '' then
    relay = req.query.relay
    local rh, rp = relay:match('^(.+):(%d+)$')
    if not rh or not self.model.valid_ip(rh) or tonumber(rp) < L.port_min
       or tonumber(rp) > L.port_max then
      return http:fail(conn, 400, 'bad_relay',
                       'relay is the host:port a TURN allocation gave this peer')
    end
  end
  local entry = { address = addr, port = port, name = cname,
                  wg_pubkey = wg_pubkey, wg_address = wg_address, relay = relay }
  -- The peer shape (doc/ICE.md 3): with ice on, a join may carry a ufrag, a
  -- pwd and up to ICE.MAX_CANDIDATES candidates -- ufrag and pwd as query
  -- fields or in a JSON body, candidates only in the body ({ufrag, pwd,
  -- candidates:[{type, address, port, protocol?}]}). The entry is keyed by
  -- ufrag, and `observed` -- the edge's own view -- is the server-reflexive
  -- candidate we supply for free and the peer cannot forge. Without a ufrag
  -- the join is the plain candidate it always was, so a WireGuard caller sees
  -- no change.
  if cfg.ice == true then
    local body = {}
    if req.body and req.body ~= '' then
      local okb, decoded = pcall(json.decode, req.body)
      if not okb or type(decoded) ~= 'table' then
        return http:fail(conn, 400, 'bad_json', 'the body is not a JSON object')
      end
      body = decoded
    end
    local ufrag = body.ufrag or req.query.ufrag
    if ufrag ~= nil then
      local pwd = body.pwd or req.query.pwd
      if type(ufrag) ~= 'string' or #ufrag < L.ufrag_min or #ufrag > L.ufrag_max
         or not ufrag:match('^[%w+/]+$') then
        return http:fail(conn, 400, 'bad_ufrag', 'ufrag is ' .. L.ufrag_min .. '-'
                         .. L.ufrag_max .. ' characters of [A-Za-z0-9+/]')
      end
      if pwd ~= nil and (type(pwd) ~= 'string' or #pwd < L.pwd_min or #pwd > L.pwd_max
                         or not pwd:match('^[%w+/]+$')) then
        return http:fail(conn, 400, 'bad_pwd', 'pwd is ' .. L.pwd_min .. '-'
                         .. L.pwd_max .. ' characters of [A-Za-z0-9+/]')
      end
      local cands = body.candidates or {}
      if type(cands) ~= 'table' then
        return http:fail(conn, 400, 'bad_candidates', 'candidates must be an array')
      end
      if #cands > ICE.MAX_CANDIDATES then
        return http:fail(conn, 400, 'too_many_candidates',
                         'at most ' .. ICE.MAX_CANDIDATES .. ' candidates per peer')
      end
      local clean = {}
      for i, c in ipairs(cands) do
        if type(c) ~= 'table' or not self.model.valid_ip(c.address)
           or type(c.port) ~= 'number' or c.port < L.port_min or c.port > L.port_max
           or c.port % 1 ~= 0
           or not ICE.CAND_TYPES[c.type]
           or (c.protocol ~= nil and c.protocol ~= 'udp' and c.protocol ~= 'tcp') then
          return http:fail(conn, 400, 'bad_candidate', 'candidate ' .. i
            .. ' needs {type: host|srflx|prflx|relay, address, port, protocol?: udp|tcp}')
        end
        clean[i] = { type = c.type, address = c.address, port = c.port, protocol = c.protocol }
        if #json.encode(clean[i]) > ICE.MAX_CANDIDATE_BYTES then
          return http:fail(conn, 400, 'bad_candidate',
                           'candidate ' .. i .. ' exceeds ' .. ICE.MAX_CANDIDATE_BYTES .. ' bytes')
        end
      end
      entry = { ufrag = ufrag, pwd = pwd, candidates = clean, name = cname,
                observed = { address = addr, port = port } }
    end
  end
  local t = self.now()
  entry.at = t
  local ttl = cfg.room_ttl or L.room_ttl
  local capacity = cfg.room_capacity or L.room_capacity
  local list, code, msg = self:room_join(row.id, room, ttl, capacity, entry, t)
  if not list then
    return http:fail(conn, code == 'rooms_saturated' and 503 or 409, code, msg)
  end
  self:traffic_bump(row.id, 'joins')
  local parts = {}
  for i, c in ipairs(list) do parts[i] = self:room_entry_json(c) end
  -- CORS for the same reason reflect carries it: a browser on the room
  -- owner's own page is half the audience for a swarm, and the door here is
  -- the pin or the key in the request, which CORS was never protecting anyway.
  return http:reply(conn, 200, '{"room":' .. json.encode(room)
    .. ',"ttl":' .. ttl
    .. ',"joined":' .. self:room_entry_json(entry)
    .. ',"candidates":[' .. table.concat(parts, ',') .. ']}', M.PUBLIC_READ)
end

-- depth: the bare-name answers ---------------------------------------------

-- report + caller: what the caller looks like from outside.
function M:serve_reflect(conn, req, label, row, d, cfg)
  local http = self.http
  local addr = http.observed_address(req)

  -- Text mode: the bare address and a newline, nothing else. This is the
  -- shape a shell wants -- `ip=$(curl -s https://name/)` -- and parsing JSON
  -- out of a router's busybox is not a thing to ask of anyone. ?format= wins
  -- over the configured default in both directions, so one name serves a
  -- script and a browser alike.
  local fmt = req.query.format or d.report_format or 'json'
  if fmt == 'text' then
    -- No observation is an empty line rather than the word 'nil': the
    -- consumer is a variable assignment, and an empty one is testable. THIS
    -- CONTRACT IS FROZEN: the bare address and a newline, nothing else,
    -- forever -- scripts assign it and it never grows.
    return http:reply(conn, 200, (addr or '') .. '\n', M.PUBLIC_READ, 'text/plain')
  end

  local prt = http.observed_port(req)
  if fmt == 'addr-port' then
    -- ADDRESS PORT and a newline; an empty line when EITHER half is
    -- unobserved. All-or-nothing on purpose: the consumer is the NAT
    -- diagnostic parsing two tokens, and half an answer would be
    -- indistinguishable from a wrong one. FROZEN, as text is.
    local line = (addr and prt) and (addr .. ' ' .. prt) or ''
    return http:reply(conn, 200, line .. '\n', M.PUBLIC_READ, 'text/plain')
  end

  return http:reply(conn, 200, {
    service = 'reflect',
    label = label,
    -- THE FILTERED CHAIN IS THE DEFAULT AND MUST STAY THE DEFAULT. The raw
    -- x-forwarded-for names every hop, and the inner ones map the
    -- deployment's own topology -- its private legs and gateway addresses --
    -- to anyone curious; a production deployment echoed an internal address
    -- to the world until public_forwarded existed. The raw chain stays
    -- reachable by the OWNER through cfg.debug_forwarded, where debugging the
    -- path is the point. Swapping the arms of this `and/or`, or reaching for
    -- req.forwarded directly because it is the shorter expression, re-opens
    -- that leak on every name that reports its caller.
    observed = { address = addr, port = prt, edge = req.edge,
                 forwarded = cfg.debug_forwarded and req.forwarded
                   or http.public_forwarded(req.forwarded) },
    -- How to measure against this deployment (diluvium-drt #25 section 1):
    -- the STUN pair and the vantage addresses, both as netcheck's own flags
    -- spell them. Only in the JSON form -- text and addr-port are frozen
    -- contracts -- and on EVERY name that reports its caller, because the
    -- measurement is the deployment's, not the canary's.
    measure = { stun = self.ice.STUN_HOSTS, vantages = self.ice.VANTAGES },
    request = { method = req.method, path = req.path,
                query = next(req.query) and req.query or nil },
    host = req.host,
    -- Presence only, permanently: reflect echoes what the caller looks like,
    -- and a credential is not a look.
    authorization = req.authorization and 'present' or 'absent',
    at = self.now(),
  }, M.PUBLIC_READ)
end

-- A report of self, or a redirect to this name's OWN address (the old
-- serve: redirect): both read the published address, both host rooms.
function M:serve_rendezvous(conn, req, label, row, d, cfg)
  local http, json = self.http, self.json

  -- ?room= reads the candidate list, under the same policy that joins: a
  -- meeting's membership is as private as its door.
  if req.query.room ~= nil then
    local room = req.query.room
    if room ~= '' then
      if not (cfg.rooms == true) then
        return http:fail(conn, 400, 'rooms_disabled',
                         'this fetchpoint does not multiplex rooms; PATCH config.rooms')
      end
      if not M.valid_room(room) then
        return http:fail(conn, 400, 'bad_room',
                         'a room is 1-64 letters, digits, hyphens or underscores')
      end
    end
    if not self:room_allowed(cfg, req) then
      -- The same failures-only bucket as the join verb: reading a room with
      -- guessed pins is the same brute force through a different door, and
      -- the two doors share one throttle.
      local sent, refused = self:refuse_authfail(conn, row)
      if refused then return sent end
      return http:fail(conn, 403, 'not_allowed', room_policy_refusal(cfg))
    end
    local ttl = cfg.room_ttl or M.LIMITS.room_ttl
    local list = self:room_list(row.id, room, ttl, self.now())
    -- Three numbers, not two, and the honesty is the design (doc/ICE.md 6):
    -- direct, relay and failed are what peers SAID; silent is a peer that
    -- joined and never said. Relay bytes the server OBSERVES arrive with TURN
    -- and are not a room's to count.
    local parts, oc = {}, { direct = 0, relay = 0, failed = 0, silent = 0 }
    -- Every member counts, not only the ICE-shaped ones. Guarding this on
    -- `c.ufrag` meant the counters answered 0/0/0/0 for a room full of
    -- WireGuard peers that had all connected -- silence reported as absence,
    -- for the one path this product ships.
    for i, c in ipairs(list) do
      parts[i] = self:room_entry_json(c)
      if c.outcome then oc[c.outcome.path] = oc[c.outcome.path] + 1
      else oc.silent = oc.silent + 1 end
    end
    -- CORS as on the join: the reader is the same browser swarm.
    return http:reply(conn, 200, '{"service":"rendezvous","label":' .. json.encode(label)
      .. ',"room":' .. json.encode(room) .. ',"ttl":' .. ttl
      .. ',"outcomes":' .. json.encode(oc)
      .. ',"candidates":[' .. table.concat(parts, ',') .. ']}', M.PUBLIC_READ)
  end

  -- The single-address read: an override is the owner's pin and wins;
  -- otherwise the last advertisement speaks. source says which, because a
  -- reader debugging "why does the name point there" deserves the answer in
  -- the answer.
  local eff, eff_at, eff_src
  if d.published_locked and d.published_address then
    -- 'override' is what the old rows and their battery say; 'locked' is the
    -- new word. One state, two spellings, until the kinds go.
    eff, eff_at, eff_src = d.published_address, row.updated_at,
                           cfg.address_override and 'override' or 'locked'
  else
    local adv = self.rows_by_name(self.db.query(
      'SELECT address, at FROM advertisement WHERE fetchpoint_id = ?', row.id))[1]
    if adv then eff, eff_at, eff_src = adv.address, adv.at, 'reported' end
  end

  -- serve = redirect: the name WORKS in a browser before DNS does. 307,
  -- because the address is temporary by nature and the method must survive
  -- (an API client POSTing at its homelab keeps its POST). Path and query
  -- forward verbatim, so a bookmark deep into the target keeps meaning what
  -- it meant. A v6 literal gets its brackets. With nothing advertised the
  -- mode falls back to the JSON answer -- a redirect to nowhere helps nobody,
  -- and the null explains itself.
  -- serve = dns publishes an A record; the name resolves straight to the
  -- owner's machine and this program never sees the request. A request that
  -- arrives here anyway got in before propagation, or by a hand-set Host --
  -- so it falls through to the JSON answer below. A redirect would be wrong:
  -- once the record lands it points at the same address the caller already
  -- reached.
  if d.behavior == 'redirect' and eff then
    local hostpart = eff:find(':', 1, true) and ('[' .. eff .. ']') or eff
    local portpart = cfg.serve_port and (':' .. cfg.serve_port) or ''
    local loc = (cfg.serve_scheme or 'http') .. '://' .. hostpart .. portpart
      .. (req.target or '/')
    return http:reply(conn, 307, { redirect = loc },
      { location = loc, ['cache-control'] = 'no-store' })
  end

  if eff then
    return http:reply(conn, 200, { service = 'rendezvous', label = label,
      advertised = { address = eff, at = eff_at, source = eff_src } }, M.PUBLIC_READ)
  end
  -- json.encode drops a nil field; the null IS the answer here, so the bytes
  -- are spelled out (the same reason the empty list is).
  return http:reply(conn, 200, '{"service":"rendezvous","label":'
    .. json.encode(label) .. ',"advertised":null}', M.PUBLIC_READ)
end

function M:serve_static_redirect(conn, req, label, row, d, cfg)
  return self.http:reply(conn, d.redirect_status or 302,
    { redirect = d.redirect_target },
    { location = d.redirect_target, ['cache-control'] = 'no-store' })
end

-- A kind the registry lists and nothing serves. 501 because the address is
-- right and the behaviour is missing -- which is exactly true, and
-- distinguishes it from the 404 above.
function M:serve_unimplemented(conn, req, label, row, d, cfg)
  return self.http:reply(conn, 501, {
    fetchpoint = { label = row.label, name = row.name, kind = row.kind,
                   fqdn = row.label .. '.' .. self.zone },
    error = { code = 'kind_not_implemented',
              message = 'this fetchpoint exists and is reachable; ' ..
                        row.kind .. ' does not do anything yet' },
  })
end

-- depth: the door ----------------------------------------------------------

function M:serve_fetchpoint(conn, req, label, verb)
  local http = self.http

  local ok, db_error = self.ready()
  if not ok then
    return http:fail(conn, 503, 'db_unavailable',
                     db_error or 'the database is not open')
  end

  -- The owner's tier rides along because the dns and join verbs meter at the
  -- OWNER's numbers: whose name it is decides how fast it may be exercised,
  -- not who is calling.
  local q = self.db.query([[SELECT f.id AS id, f.label AS label, f.name AS name,
                              f.kind AS kind, f.config AS config,
                              f.template AS template, f.source AS source, f.access AS access, f.channel AS channel, f.behavior AS behavior, f.subject AS subject, f.published_address AS published_address, f.published_locked AS published_locked, f.redirect_target AS redirect_target, f.redirect_status AS redirect_status, f.report_format AS report_format, f.serve_relay_config AS serve_relay_config,
                              f.created_at AS created_at, f.updated_at AS updated_at,
                              a.tier AS tier
                         FROM fetchpoint f
                         JOIN account a ON a.id = f.owner_account_id
                        WHERE f.label = ? AND f.deleted_at IS NULL LIMIT 1]], label)
  local row = self.rows_by_name(q)[1]

  -- A label that was never allocated and one whose FetchPoint was deleted
  -- answer identically, on purpose. The label table keeps released names
  -- forever precisely so a stale resolver answer cannot reach a new owner;
  -- saying "this used to exist" here would hand back the existence
  -- information that arrangement exists to withhold.
  if not row then
    return http:fail(conn, 404, 'no_fetchpoint',
                     'no fetchpoint answers to ' .. label .. '.' .. self.zone)
  end
  local kind = self.model.KINDS[row.kind]
  -- The dimensions decide the bare-name answer below; `kind` still gates the
  -- verbs and names the config registry, and both of those move next.
  local d, cfg = self.model.DIM.of(row)

  if verb then
    if not (kind and kind.verbs and kind.verbs[verb]) then
      return http:fail(conn, 404, 'no_verb', row.kind .. ' has no verb ' .. verb)
    end
    return self[M.VERB[verb] or M.VERB_DEFAULT](self, conn, req, label, row, d, cfg, verb)
  end

  -- Every bare-name request counts as a read, whatever its outcome: this
  -- counter answers "is anyone out there", and a refused read is still
  -- someone out there. denied counts the refusals among them.
  self:traffic_bump(row.id, 'reads')

  if d.behavior == 'report' and d.subject == 'caller' then
    return self:serve_reflect(conn, req, label, row, d, cfg)
  end
  if (d.behavior == 'report' and d.subject == 'self')
     or (d.behavior == 'redirect' and not d.redirect_target) then
    return self:serve_rendezvous(conn, req, label, row, d, cfg)
  end
  if d.behavior == 'redirect' then
    return self:serve_static_redirect(conn, req, label, row, d, cfg)
  end
  return self:serve_unimplemented(conn, req, label, row, d, cfg)
end

return M
